.TH EX 1
.SH NAME
ex \- text editor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ex
[
.B \-
] [
.B \-v
] [
.B \-t
tag ] [
.B \-r
] [
.BI + command
] [
.B \-l
] name ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ex
is a line editor contained within
.BR vi (1).
.B ex
is a superset of another
VENIX editor
.BR ed (1).
Most users start off with
.BR vi ,
and then use
.B ex
at
their convenience.
Or, you can start editing in
.BR ex ,
and then go into
.B vi
by
typing ``\fB:vi\fR''.
.PP
See ``\fIAn Introduction to Display Editing with Vi\fR'' in the
.I "User Guide"
for
more information about the display editor
.BR vi .
.SH FILES
.nf
.ta 2.2i
/usr/lib/ex*strings	error messages
/etc/termcap	describes capabilities of terminals
/tmp/Ex\fInnnnn\fR	editor temporary
/tmp/Rx\fInnnnn\fR	named buffer temporary
/usr/preserve	preservation directory
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
awk(1), ed(1), grep(1), sed(1), vi(1), termcap(5), environ(5)
.SH BUGS
The
.B undo
command causes all marks to be lost on lines changed and
then restored if the marked lines were changed.
.PP
.B undo
never clears the buffer modified condition.
.PP
The
.B z
command prints a number of logical rather than physical lines.
More than a screen full of output may result if long lines are present.
.PP
File input/output errors don't print a name if the command line `\-'
option is used.
.PP
There is no easy way to do a single scan ignoring case.
.PP
The editor does not warn if text is placed in named buffers and not used
before exiting the editor.
.PP
Null characters are discarded in input files, and cannot appear in resultant
files.
.sp 2
.ce
.B "Entering/leaving ex"
.PP
.nf
.ta 2i
% \fBex\fI name\fR	edit \fIname\fR, start at end
% \fBex +\fIn name\fR	... at line \fIn\fR
% \fBex \-t\fI tag\fR	start at \fItag\fR
% \fBex\fI name ...\fR	edit first; rest via \fB:n\fR
% \fBex \-R\fI name\fR	read only mode
\fB: x\fR	exit, saving changes
\fB: q!\fR	exit, discarding changes
.fi
.sp
.ce
.B "Ex states"
.TP 1.4i
Command
Normal and initial state.
Input
prompted for by
.BR : .
Your kill character
cancels partial command.
.TP
Insert
Entered by
.B "a i"
and
.BR c .
Arbitrary text
then terminates with line having only
.B .
character on it or abnormally with interrupt.
.TP
Open/visual
Entered by
.B open
or
.BR vi ,
terminates with
.B Q
or ^\e.
.sp
